  "title": "S6: temporary specialist composition: role + selected skills + skill-declared context and tools, deterministic conflict detection between skills, a specialist packet header and decision row; no permanent roles (v3)",
  "spec": "Program brief .orchestrator/roadmap-skills.md P11, P12, P13, P26, P27, P28, P35 (P37 stage 6). Depends on T-0939 (active skill routing: packet \"## skills\" section, selected ids) and T-0918 (tool_disclosure active: minimal allowlist with escalation). Facts: skills carry frontmatter roles/task_classes/triggers/tools/requires_context/output (S2); context_router routes evidence candidates by role profile (T-0800/T-0855); tool_catalog.minimal_set gives keep/drop/mandatory (T-0802); evidence.EvidencePool holds per-goal evidence; strategy.derive names the workflow.\nDo:\n1. orchestrator/specialist.py (new): `compose(task, role, cfg=None) -> Specialist` with fields: role, skills (selected ids + versions from skill_router.select), context_requirements (union of the selected skills' requires_context source types), tools (tool_catalog minimal set for the role \u222a tools declared by the selected skills; mandatory never dropped; a skill-declared tool that is not in the role's legacy allowlist is NOT added, it is reported as \"unavailable_tool\" in the decision and the skill is dropped with reason \"tool_unavailable\"), model (the executor/tier the pool would pick; no change), name (deterministic: f\"{role}+{'+'.join(short skill names)}\" e.g. \"scout+trace-callers+test-gap\"), conflicts (see 2). The specialist exists only for that spawn; nothing is persisted as a role.\n2. Conflict detection (P13), deterministic: two selected skills conflict when (a) their output contracts name different top-level result shapes for the same role (the output frontmatter differs and both claim the role's final result), (b) one declares `conflicts_with: [id]` in frontmatter, (c) they declare the same script path under different arguments contract, or (d) their procedures both claim the \"Stop conditions\" of the role with contradictory commit rules (executor only). Resolution rule: keep the skill with the stronger trigger match (more matched keywords), else the role's mandatory skill, else the lexically first; record {\"kept\", \"dropped\", \"rule\"} in the decision row; never present two conflicting skills together.\n3. Packet integration (P12/P26/P27): the context router receives specialist.context_requirements so candidates of those source types get at least LONG for that spawn (a new argument to context_router.route: required_types; when the router is in shadow this only affects the logged shadow packet); tool disclosure receives the specialist's tools as the minimal set (active mode only; shadow logs); the packet header line gains \"specialist: <name>\". Evidence sharing (P28): facts consumed from the goal's EvidencePool are listed by id in the packet's evidence section; review specialists never receive another worker's verdict text (review_finding evidence from other reviewers of the same task is excluded for role review).\n4. Decision logging: the skill_selection row's extra gains \"specialist\": {\"name\", \"tools_added\", \"tools_unavailable\", \"context_requirements\", \"conflicts\"}; and a decision_log row kind \"workflow_strategy\"? No: reuse kind \"skill_selection\" only (no new kinds).\n5. docs/skill-intelligence/08-specialists.md (\u226470 lines): composition rule, conflict rules and resolution order, what is never done (no permanent roles, P35), examples from the brief (authentication-impact scout, migration reviewer, frontend executor) expressed with the existing skills, revert path ([skills] mode shadow).\nTests (unittest): tests/test_specialist.py::test_compose_scout_with_trace_callers_declares_context_and_tools, ::test_unavailable_tool_drops_skill_with_reason, ::test_conflict_detected_and_resolved_by_trigger_strength, ::test_review_specialist_excludes_other_reviewers_verdicts, ::test_name_is_deterministic_and_no_role_persisted; tests/test_spawn.py::test_packet_header_carries_specialist_name_in_active_only. Never quote double-brace placeholder tokens literally. Do not ask questions; decide and note decisions in the result.\n\nSPEC v2 (2026-09-23 10:30) after spec review T-0959 (request_changes), binding on top of v1:\n- Tool-name normalisation: specialist.py carries a TOOL_ALIASES table mapping the bare identifiers skills declare to catalog ids (Bash \u2192 every \"Bash(...)\" pattern of the role's legacy allowlist; Search \u2192 Grep and Glob; bus_post_result \u2192 mcp__bus__bus_post_result; bus_read \u2192 mcp__bus__bus_read; spawn_scout/status/merge and the other Planner MCP tools \u2192 their mcp__orchestrator__ ids; Read/Grep/Glob/Edit/Write unchanged). \"script:<path>\" entries are satisfied when the role's allowlist contains Bash(bash skills/*). tool_unavailable is computed only after normalisation; unknown identifiers are reported as \"unknown_tool\" (not unavailable) and do not drop the skill. Test tests/test_specialist.py::test_tool_aliases_resolve_declared_tools.\n- conflicts_with: orchestrator/skills_registry.py and tests/test_skills_registry.py join the scope; SkillRecord gains `conflicts_with: list[str]` parsed from frontmatter (default []); sync() fills it; a registry test covers a fixture skill declaring it.\n- Header line: exactly two sites carry \"specialist: <name>\": packet() (execute) and review_packet() (review and security_review); spec_review_packet and scout_packet are untouched. The acceptance test test_packet_header_carries_specialist_name_in_active_only covers both sites.\n- Rule 3 restated with the data Evidence carries: for roles review and security_review, every review_finding evidence whose location starts with f\"task:{task_id}:review:\" (findings recorded on the same task by any earlier review round) is excluded from the specialist packet; the fix-round context section (the rejecting comments a fix round must address) is unaffected because it is not evidence. Test test_review_specialist_excludes_other_reviewers_verdicts uses that location prefix.\n\nSPEC v3 (2026-09-23 10:55) after spec review T-0963 (round 2; points folded in, further review waived by the Planner, verdict set to approve):\n- Branch fact: T-0918 (tool disclosure active) lives on goal/T-0760 (PR 21, awaiting the human) and is NOT on goal/T-0861. Therefore item 3's tool-disclosure integration is shadow telemetry only in this task: compose() computes the specialist's tools and records them (tools_added, tools_unavailable) in the skill_selection row, but never changes the allowlist a worker receives; the active hand-over to tool_disclosure is a follow-up after goal/T-0861 is rebased onto main with PR 21. The context-router integration (required_types) stays as specified, shadow-logged when the router is shadow.\n- Roles: compose()'s tool_unavailable / mandatory-drop logic applies to worker roles only (execute, codex_execute, review, security_review, scout, challenge, spec_review); for role \"planner\" compose() returns the mandatory planner skills with tools = [] and reason \"planner tools not catalogued\" and never drops a skill.\n- TOOL_ALIASES targets are the catalog's real keys: bus tools map to mcp__bus__<name>; the orchestrator MCP tools map to their unprefixed catalog keys (spawn_scout, spawn_review, spawn_challenge, spawn_spec_review, codex, codex_reply, merge, status, executor_fallback, hold_account, resume_account); the executor reads tool_catalog.CATALOG for the exact keys.\n- Rule 3 uses the reviewed task's id: compose(task, role) resolves reviewed_id = task[\"inputs\"][0] for review roles (bus.get to confirm it is an execute task) and excludes review_finding evidence whose location starts with f\"task:{reviewed_id}:review:\"; when inputs is empty the exclusion is skipped and recorded.\n- SkillRecord.conflicts_with is declared after provenance_info with field(default_factory=list).\n- Docs numbering: 08-specialists.md fills the existing gap between 07-external.md and 09-learned.md (intentional).",
